    <p class="eyebrow">Foundation 5</p>

    <h1>Measurement and Limits</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      The proposed system depends on measuring causal contribution without
      pretending that causal inference is perfect. Its credibility therefore
      rests as much on representing uncertainty and error honestly as on
      producing an attribution estimate.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Core question:</strong>
      What can a provenance system measure reliably enough to support
      analysis or institutional action, and what remains uncertain?
    </div>

    <h2 id="measurement-problem">The measurement problem</h2>

    <p>
      An economic outcome can have many interacting causes. Skill, effort,
      prior education, inherited resources, network position, geography,
      institutional access, market conditions, and the contributions of
      other participants may all influence the result.
    </p>

    <p>
      Measurement is therefore not a simple exercise in attaching one
      outcome to one cause. It is a causal-attribution problem involving
      incomplete observation, interaction effects, changing conditions,
      and counterfactual questions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="two-levels">Two related levels of analysis</h2>

    <div class="grid">
      <section class="card">
        <h3>Economic provenance auditing</h3>

        <p>
          Estimates how much an economic position or reward reflects
          meritocratic and non-meritocratic inputs, using available
          administrative, statistical, geographic, educational, and
          network information.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Contribution decomposition</h3>

        <p>
          Estimates how identifiable contributions relate to measurable
          system outcomes, including marginal effects, interactions,
          persistence over time, and approximation error.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <p>
      The two levels share a common discipline: both produce estimates
      rather than perfect reconstructions, and both require the strength
      of any conclusion to reflect the quality of the evidence.
    </p>

    <h2 id="audit">Provenance auditing</h2>

    <p>
      The MPS foundations define provenance auditing as a probabilistic
      reconstruction of the causal inputs to an economic outcome. The
      objective is not to recover every event in an individual history
      with certainty. The objective is to create a sufficiently reliable
      estimate for a stated analytical or institutional purpose.
    </p>

    <p>
      The proposed Advantage Index represents an estimated proportion of
      an economic return attributed to non-meritocratic inputs. It is not
      presented as direct observation of moral desert.
    </p>

    <h2 id="evidence">Potential evidence categories</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>administrative and tax records;</li>
      <li>educational attainment and access information;</li>
      <li>intergenerational income information;</li>
      <li>geographic and institutional conditions;</li>
      <li>professional-network information; and</li>
      <li>
        documented familial wealth transfers where available.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
      <strong>Boundary:</strong>
      Availability of a variable does not establish that the variable is
      causal, morally relevant, complete, or appropriate for an individual
      decision. Data selection and interpretation remain part of the
      measurement problem.
    </div>

    <h2 id="decomposition">Contribution decomposition</h2>

    <p>
      The technical framework defines a qualifying contribution through
      three primary conditions:
    </p>

    <div class="grid">
      <section class="card">
        <h3>Identifiability</h3>

        <p>
          The contribution can be traced to a contributor.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Materiality</h3>

        <p>
          The contribution has a measurable effect on downstream outputs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Persistence</h3>

        <p>
          The contribution retains non-zero influence over time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Context</h3>

        <p>
          The contribution may depend on other inputs and should not
          automatically be treated as isolated.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <h2 id="sets">Why contributions are measured in sets</h2>

    <p>
      Contributions can interact, compound, and form ensembles. The
      framework consequently defines value over sets of contributions
      rather than assuming that every input acts independently.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      V(S), where S is a set of contributions
    </div>

    <p>
      This creates a cooperative-game structure, but the practical setting
      differs from a small static game. Contributor populations can be
      large, marginal effects can depend on context, and the value function
      can change as the system evolves.
    </p>

    <h2 id="marginal">Approximate marginal contribution</h2>

    <p>
      A basic marginal-contribution question asks how system value changes
      when a contribution is absent:
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      MC(cᵢ, S) = V(S) − V(S \ {cᵢ})
    </div>

    <p>
      In an AI system, a contribution may be embedded in training data,
      model weights, and interacting behaviors. Removing it cleanly may be
      infeasible. The proposal therefore uses perturbation-based estimation,
      examining performance changes when a contribution is masked, modified,
      or down-weighted.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      MĈ(cᵢ, S) =
      estimated change in performance when cᵢ is perturbed
    </div>

    <div class="warning">
      <strong>Important:</strong>
      The estimated marginal contribution is an estimator of the true
      causal effect. It carries approximation error and depends on the
      selected model, perturbation, benchmark, and performance metric.
    </div>

    <h2 id="interaction">Interaction and synergy</h2>

    <p>
      Two contributions may produce more value together than either
      produces separately. The framework represents this interaction
      with a synergy term:
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      Syn(cᵢ, cⱼ) =
      V({cᵢ, cⱼ}) − V({cᵢ}) − V({cⱼ})
    </div>

    <p>
      Pairwise synergy is an interpretive tool. The broader allocation
      problem still concerns the distribution of total value across a set
      of contributors, not only isolated two-contribution interactions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="time">Persistence and time</h2>

    <p>
      Contribution value may continue, decay, or change as models and
      downstream systems evolve. A persistence function represents the
      probability that a contribution continues to influence outputs at
      a later time.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      P(cᵢ, t) =
      modeled probability that cᵢ influences outputs at time t
    </div>

    <p>
      The proposed present-value treatment combines estimated marginal
      contribution, discounting, and persistence:
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      PV(cᵢ) =
      ∫₀∞ MĈ(cᵢ, Sₜ) · e<sup>−ρt</sup> · P(cᵢ, t) dt
    </div>

    <p>
      This is a model of time-dependent value, not a guarantee that the
      contribution will continue to generate a return.
    </p>

    <h2 id="royalty">Royalty-flow policy parameters</h2>

    <p>
      The technical proposal combines direct contribution value and
      modeled synergy in a royalty-flow equation:
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      Rᵢ = α · PV(cᵢ) + β · Σⱼ≠ᵢ Syn(cᵢ, cⱼ)
    </div>

    <p>
      The coefficients α and β are policy parameters rather than values
      forced by the mathematics. They must be chosen so aggregate
      royalties do not exceed total value unless an external subsidy or
      funding mechanism is introduced.
    </p>

    <div class="formula">
      Σᵢ Rᵢ ≤ V(C)
    </div>

    <h2 id="computation">Computational feasibility</h2>

    <p>
      Exact Shapley-style computation grows exponentially with the number
      of contributors. The source materials therefore propose approximation
      strategies rather than exact enumeration at scale:
    </p>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Sampling-based Shapley approximation</strong>
        over selected contribution orderings.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Influence-function perturbation</strong>
        to estimate marginal effects without complete retraining.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Gradient-based attribution</strong>
        for differentiable model classes.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Model-agnostic explainability methods</strong>
        where model-specific techniques are unavailable.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Domain-specific valuation kernels</strong>
        designed around the evidence and performance measures of a
        particular field.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Computational tractability does not by itself establish causal
      validity. An efficient approximation can still estimate the wrong
      construct if the benchmark, data, or model is poorly specified.
    </p>

    <h2 id="counterfactual">The counterfactual problem</h2>

    <p>
      The hardest question is what would have happened without a
      particular contribution or advantage. That counterfactual is
      normally unobservable, and the relevant inputs can operate through
      interacting mechanisms that are difficult to isolate.
    </p>

    <p>
      The institutional framework proposes population-level statistical
      inference and comparison among relevantly similar cases as a
      practical substitute for perfect individual counterfactual
      reconstruction. That approach still depends on model specification,
      available variables, and the plausibility of the comparison.
    </p>

    <h2 id="error">Sources of error</h2>

    <div class="grid">
      <section class="card">
        <h3>Omitted variables</h3>

        <p>
          Important causal inputs may be unavailable, unmeasured, or
          excluded.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Proxy error</h3>

        <p>
          A measurable indicator may imperfectly represent the underlying
          concept.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Model error</h3>

        <p>
          The selected statistical or computational model may misrepresent
          relationships.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Benchmark error</h3>

        <p>
          The performance metric may reward the wrong outcome or fail to
          capture quality.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Interaction error</h3>

        <p>
          Attribution may miss nonlinear, higher-order, or changing
          contribution effects.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h3>Temporal error</h3>

        <p>
          Persistence and decay assumptions may not match actual influence
          over time.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <h2 id="confidence">Scale action to confidence</h2>

    <p>
      Epistemic humility is an operational rule, not merely a disclaimer.
      Where the evidence is incomplete or proxy-based, the proposed
      response should be weaker than where the evidence is direct, robust,
      and independently verifiable.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
      <strong>
        Stronger evidence can support stronger conclusions. Greater
        uncertainty requires narrower claims, wider error bounds, more
        review, and more reversible action.
      </strong>
    </div>

    <h2 id="integrity">Record integrity is not factual truth</h2>

    <p>
      Cryptographic anchoring can make later alteration detectable and can
      bind a record to an identity or submission event. It cannot, by
      itself, prove that the original claim was accurate, complete,
      lawfully obtained, or causally relevant.
    </p>

    <p>
      External evidence, validation, challenge procedures, recalculation,
      and logged correction cycles remain necessary.
    </p>

    <h2 id="validation">What validation would require</h2>

    <p>
      A production-ready implementation would need evidence beyond a
      formal model. Relevant validation questions include:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Do contribution estimates remain stable across reasonable model
        choices?
      </li>

      <li>
        Do independent evaluators reproduce the result?
      </li>

      <li>
        How large are approximation and sampling errors?
      </li>

      <li>
        Do the selected metrics predict the outcomes the system claims
        to value?
      </li>

      <li>
        Can disputes and corrections be resolved without obscuring the
        audit history?
      </li>

      <li>
        Do incentive, privacy, and administrative costs remain acceptable
        in practice?
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="not-established">What is not yet established</h2>

    <p>
      The source materials describe a proposed mathematical and
      institutional architecture. They do not establish production-scale
      empirical validation, universal cross-domain accuracy, or error-free
      individual attribution.
    </p>

    <p>
      The public claim should therefore remain bounded: the framework
      identifies methods for estimating contribution and advantage under
      uncertainty, together with design principles for exposing and
      responding to that uncertainty.
    </p>

    <div class="status">
      <strong>Status:</strong>
      Proposed mathematical and institutional architecture. Approximation
      methods and limitations are described; production-scale empirical
      validation is not established in the supplied materials.
    </div>

